course: biology
lesson: 6
title: The Arctic: ice, snow and the polar bear's world
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Arctic is the frozen top of the world: an ocean covered by floating sea ice, surrounded by the cold treeless land called the tundra in the north of Europe, Asia and North America.
explain: Because the Earth is tilted, north of the Arctic Circle the sun does not set for weeks in midsummer and does not rise for weeks in midwinter, so on a trip to northern Norway it can still shine at 11 pm.
explain: Arctic animals are built like thermos flasks: thick fur and fat, small ears, short legs and snouts, and tricks like sleeping under a bushy tail or living in tunnels under the snow blanket.
explain: The polar bear is the king of the sea ice: it hunts seals from the ice edge, so as the world warms and the summer ice shrinks, it has less time to hunt.
explain: In Frozen, Sven the reindeer begs for carrots; real reindeer dig through the snow with their broad hooves to eat lichen, and unlike other deer, both the males and the females grow antlers.
fact: Polar bears hunt seals from the sea ice and swim well, kept warm by fat up to about 10 cm thick; their skin is black and their hairs are see-through, only looking white because they scatter the light, like snow does.
fact: Polar bears are classed as marine mammals, and as Arctic sea ice shrinks in summer they have less time to hunt and must live longer on their fat.
fact: The Arctic fox sleeps with its bushy tail over its nose, has small ears, short legs and very thick fur, and turns white in winter and brown or grey in summer, so it matches the snow or the bare ground.
fact: Walruses use their tusks, up to about 1 metre long, to haul out onto ice and to show who is boss, and find clams on the seabed with sensitive whiskers.
fact: Reindeer, called caribou in North America, have broad hooves for snow and ice, dig for lichen in winter, and their eyes change from gold in summer to blue in winter.
fact: North of the Arctic Circle the sun does not set for weeks in midsummer and does not rise for weeks in midwinter, because the Earth is tilted.
fact: Snowy owls often hunt in daylight on the tundra; one may eat more than 1,500 lemmings in a year, and they lay more eggs when lemmings are plentiful.
fact: The male narwhal's tusk is a front tooth up to about 3 metres long that grows through its upper lip, and in the Middle Ages such tusks were sold as unicorn horns.
fact: When wolves come, musk oxen form a ring with horns out and calves in the middle, and their woolly underfur, qiviut, is said to be about eight times warmer than sheep's wool.
fact: The northern lights glow when tiny particles from the sun hit gases high in the air, mostly green from oxygen, and near Antarctica there are southern lights too.
fact: Lemmings spend the winter in tunnels under the snow, which traps air like a blanket; the story that they jump off cliffs together is a myth helped by a faked 1958 film.
fact: The bowhead whale can smash through ice about 60 cm thick with its head to make a hole to breathe, has blubber up to about 50 cm thick, and some are thought to have lived over 200 years.
fact: Reindeer are the only deer in which both males and females usually grow antlers.
exercise: Where do polar bears hunt seals? | From the edges of the Arctic sea ice | In the thick pine forests of northern Canada | On the thick ice of Antarctica, far to the south | At the very bottom of the deep, dark sea
exercise: What colour is a polar bear's skin? | Black | White | Pink | Grey with white spots
exercise: Why does a polar bear's fur look white? | Its see-through hairs scatter light | Each hair is painted white by falling snow | Its fur is white like a sheep's wool | It rolls in the snow every day to turn white
exercise: How does an Arctic fox keep its face warm while it sleeps? | It wraps its bushy tail over its nose like a scarf | It sleeps with its head buried deep in the snow | It tucks its face under a polar bear | It keeps its mouth open wide to breathe in warm air from its belly
exercise: Why does the Arctic fox turn brown or grey in summer? | To match the bare ground when the snow melts | Because it gets dirty from rolling in the summer mud | Because it is getting old | To warn other foxes away
exercise: What does a walrus use its tusks for? | To haul itself onto ice and to show who is boss | To dig up clams from the seabed like a pair of spades | To spear fish like a harpoon | To crack open clam shells like a pair of nutcrackers
exercise: How does a walrus find clams on the dark seabed? | With its very sensitive whiskers | With its huge eyes that see in the dark | By making echolocation clicks like a dolphin | By the smell of the clams in the water
exercise: What do reindeer mainly eat in winter? | Lichen they dig out from under the snow | Carrots left out for them by kind people | Fish they catch through holes in the frozen ice | Pine needles they pull from tall forest trees
exercise: What do people in North America call reindeer? | Caribou | Moose | Elk | Musk ox
exercise: Why can the sun still shine at 11 pm in northern Norway in summer? | The Earth is tilted, so the far north leans towards the sun | The sun moves much closer to Norway in the summer months | Norway is the closest country to the sun | The clocks in Norway are set wrong
exercise: Why do snowy owls often hunt in daylight? | In the Arctic summer it is light almost all the time | They are blind in the dark | Lemmings come out only at noon, when it is warmest | They are afraid of the bright northern lights at night
exercise: What is a narwhal's tusk? | A very long front tooth that grows through its upper lip | A horn growing from its forehead like a unicorn's, made of bone | A bone of its nose | A spike of ice frozen to its head
exercise: What do musk oxen do when wolves come? | Form a ring with horns facing out and calves in the middle | Run away as fast as they can in all directions across the snow | Lie down and pretend to be dead | Hide their calves in snow holes
exercise: What makes the northern lights glow? | Particles from the sun hitting gases high in the air | Sunlight reflecting off the snow and ice into the sky | Huge fires on the ice | Light from the Moon bouncing off the clouds high above the Arctic
exercise: Why do lemmings live under the snow in winter? | The snow traps air and is much warmer than the wind above | They are hiding from the sun | There is fresh grass growing on top of the snow | It is a race to dig the longest tunnel before spring comes
exercise: How does a bowhead whale breathe when the sea is frozen over? | It smashes through the ice with its head | It holds its breath all winter long under the ice | It breathes through gills under the ice, like a fish | It follows the big ships that break the ice
exercise: Why is shrinking sea ice bad for polar bears? | They hunt seals from the ice, so they get less time to hunt | Polar bears need ice to drink from | They sleep only on floating ice and drown without it | Seals eat polar bears when there is no ice left for them to stand on
test: «in frozen sven eats carrots so reindeer live on carrots» What is right? | No, wild reindeer dig through snow for lichen and graze plants | Yes, carrots are their main food | Yes, and they pull carrots out of the frozen ground with their antlers | No, reindeer eat only fish
test: «girl reindeer don't have antlers, only santa's boys do» What is right? | Wrong: in reindeer both males and females usually grow antlers | Right: only males have antlers, and they use them to pull the sleigh | Right: no reindeer has antlers | Wrong: only females have antlers
test: «lemmings all jump off cliffs, saw it in an old film» What is right? | A myth: that 1958 film faked the scene | True: they all do it every single winter | True: they really think that they can fly | True, but only young lemmings do it in spring
test: «the narwhal's a real unicorn innit» What is right? | No, its tusk is a long tooth, though old traders sold it as unicorn horn | Yes, it is a unicorn that lives in the sea | Yes, its horn grows from its forehead, just like a unicorn's in a fairy tale | No, the tusk is made of ice
test: Count them: a walrus with 2 tusks and three narwhal males with 1 tusk each. How many tusks? | 5 | 4 | 6 | 3
test: What would happen to an Arctic fox that stayed white all summer on brown tundra? | Its prey and predators would spot it more easily | Nothing, white is invisible everywhere | It would stay cooler in the sun and catch more food | It would turn into a snowy owl
test: «polar bear's white so its skin's white under, obviously» What is right? | No, its skin is black and its hairs are see-through | Yes, white skin and white hair, all over | No, its skin is pink like a pig's | No, its skin is striped
test: «why do musk oxen just stand in a circle like idiots when wolves come» Which is the best answer? | The wall of horns protects the calves in the middle | They are too slow to do anything else but stand | They are counting the wolves | They are trying to look bigger to each other
test: «snow's freezing, how come lemmings live in it all winter» Which is the best answer? | Snow traps air and works like a blanket, warmer than the wind above | Lemmings cannot feel cold | They eat the snow to keep warm | They sleep all winter in deep caves under the mountains, far from the snow
test: «sun at 11 at night?? norway's broken lol» Which is the best answer? | The Earth's tilt keeps the far north in sunlight all day in midsummer | Norway is closer to the sun | The sun stops moving in summer | The northern lights are so bright that they make it look like the sun is still up
test: «whale that lives 200 years? pull the other one» What is right? | Some bowhead whales are thought to have lived over 200 years | No whale lives longer than 20 years | All whales live exactly 100 years | Only whales in zoos and aquariums live that long, with vets
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the skin and fur of the polar bear? | The skin of the polar bear is black, whereas its hairs are translucent and merely appear white by the scattering of light | The skin and the hairs of the polar bear are alike white, the animal being white throughout | The skin of the polar bear is pink and its hairs are white | The polar bear possesses no fur whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the Sun remains visible at a late hour in northern Norway during midsummer. | The axis of the Earth is inclined, whereby the far north is directed towards the Sun | The Sun approaches nearer to Norway during summer than to any other country of Europe | Norway is situated nearer to the Sun than any other country | The clocks of Norway are improperly adjusted
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alleged mass suicide of lemmings. | The notion is a myth, reinforced by a film of 1958 in which the scene was contrived | Lemmings leap from cliffs in great numbers each winter | Lemmings leap from cliffs only in years of abundance, when their numbers exceed the food | The phenomenon has been observed by every Arctic explorer
exam: Ascertain the defensive conduct of musk oxen upon the approach of wolves. | The herd forms a ring with horns directed outward and the calves enclosed within | The herd disperses in every direction | Each animal lies motionless upon the snow | The calves are concealed in burrows excavated beneath the snow
